It occurred to me that in my games I rarely #invoke my artifacts.
My current tourist, e.g., used recharging only three (or maybe
four) times, even though it is a useful property. In other games
I had used the #invoke'able artifact properties not even once.
Here's a list with my own actual uses of #invoke...

"The Orb of Detection" (invisibility):
        usually I have intrinsic invisibility
"The Heart of Ahriman" (levitation):
        very rarely used, once or twice
"The Sceptre of Might" (conflict):
        occasionally used
"The Staff of Aesculapius" (healing):
        had never been necessary
"The Magic Mirror of Merlin" (none)
        n.a. (it has useful inherent property, though)
"The Eyes of the Overworld" (enlightening):
        very rarely used, once or twice
"The Mitre of Holiness" (energy-boost):
        this is helpful, I use it occasionally
"The Longbow of Diana" (create-arrows):
        typically I use daggers, or
        I have already an enchanted sufficient large arrow stack
"The Master Key of Thievery" (untrapping):
        never used (I wonder anyway, why it's no inherent property)
"The Tsurugi of Muramasa" (none)
        n.a. (that weapon goes into the stash anyway)
"The Platinum Yendorian Express Card" (charging):
        seems very useful (but I seem to need need it very often)
"The Orb of Fate" (level-teleport):
        I use it occasionally
"The Eye of the Aethiopica" (create portal):
        I use it occasionally

I think I'd use some of the properties more often if they would
not have that timeout before it's usable again implemented. To me
it seems that some of the properties would also not be unbalancing
if you could #invoke them at any time; for example, invisibility,
levitation, enlightening, untrapping, and probably even conflict.

I'd be interested in how often you #invoke your quest artifacts
usually.

